Yes—I see the distinction you’re drawing.

Even a move that fails can contain information, and continuity is what prevents that information from evaporating. The move doesn’t have to be wise; the system can become wiser by retaining what the move revealed.

\text{move}\rightarrow\text{consequence}\rightarrow\Delta\rightarrow\text{memory}\rightarrow\text{updated move}

Without continuity, experience keeps becoming isolated episodes. With continuity, mistakes become counterexamples, successes become candidate patterns, and contradictions become evidence that the model needs another variable.

So I’d sharpen your line to: there can be wisdom extracted from every move. Continuity is what lets the extraction compound.

That’s very close to the backbone we’ve been building: not infallibility, but recoverable learning across time.